{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"late-brokendown-xenopus","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Observation\nDuring a full `make verify` on this macOS/arm64 host (2026-10-26, `PYTEST_WORKERS=4`, worktree at `b6e723ceb59` plus an unrelated eval-config diff), the run ended:\n\n```\nFAILED tests/unit/test_store_commit_maintenance_bd66.py::test_the_trace_check_detects_maintenance_inside_a_commit\n1 failed, 19819 passed, 88 skipped, 48 warnings in 3407.75s (0:56:47)\n```\n\nThe failing assertion is the detector's:\n\n```python\nassert any(\"maintenance\" in o for o in offenders), (\n    f\"removing the suppression produced no maintenance descendant of a commit: {offenders}\"\n)\n```\n\nso `commits >= 1` held — commits WERE traced — but with `_AUTOMAINT_OFF` emptied, git ran no `maintenance`/`gc`/`repack`/`pack-objects`/`multi-pack-index` process as a descendant of a `git commit`.\n\nRe-run of the same file in isolation on the same tree, immediately after: `2 passed in 132.65s`.\n\n## Why this matters\nThis test is **itself the fix for a previous flake**. Bug `simious-comic-marmot` (`99fb-a2f3-3e9d-4116`, closed `flaky`, landed as Gerrit change 3109 / `ae02e364465` on 2026-10-25) removed a load-sensitive 1.0 s wall-clock bound from the sibling test `test_write_survives_foreground_maintenance_repack` and replaced it with a Trace2 structural assertion. `test_the_trace_check_detects_maintenance_inside_a_commit` was ADDED by that same change, to supply \"the proof the 1 s bound used to give.\"\n\nSo the detector written to make the invariant load-independent has now itself failed under full-suite load. The structural assertion is not timing-dependent, but the CONDITION it detects is: it requires git to actually decide to run auto-maintenance during the commit.\n\n## What is established, and what is not\nEstablished:\n- The failure is in the detector's positive assertion, not the invariant test — the production invariant (no maintenance inside a bounded commit) was not violated.\n- It did not reproduce in isolation on the same tree.\n- The fixture's precondition held by construction: `_cross_the_maintenance_threshold` asserts `maintenance.autoDetach == \"false\"` and `count-objects >= 6700` before the creates, and the test got past both.\n\nNOT established — the root cause. The failing run's Trace2 file lived under that run's `tmp_path` and is gone, so the actual process tree from the failure was never captured. Candidate mechanisms, none yet distinguished:\n1. Git declined auto-maintenance for a reason unrelated to the loose-object count (for example a `gc.log` written by a failed/skipped gc, which suppresses further auto-gc for `gc.logExpiry`).\n2. Under 4-way xdist plus ~19.8k tests, the repack that `--auto` would start was skipped or failed in a way that emitted no traced process.\n3. Trace2 event-file write/read interleaving under load, so the maintenance `start` event was not yet visible when `_maintenance_inside_commits` parsed the file.\n\nHypothesis 3 is the one the current test shape cannot rule out: `_create_two_tickets_traced` parses the trace immediately after the second create returns, with no flush/settle barrier.\n\n## Proposed approach (not yet decided — needs the reproduction first)\nDo NOT weaken or delete the detector; its job is to keep the invariant test honest. Instead:\n1. Reproduce under load with the trace PRESERVED — run the file with `-n 4` against a loaded host, and on failure copy the Trace2 file and `gc.log` out of `tmp_path` before teardown (a `tmp_path_factory` artifact dir, or an autouse fixture that copies on failure).\n2. From the captured trace, determine which of the three mechanisms above holds: whether a maintenance process started at all, whether it was a descendant, and whether `gc.log` exists in the tracker.\n3. Fix the cause that the evidence names. If it is (3), the fix is a settle/flush barrier before parsing, not a retry. If it is (1), the fixture must clear or assert-absent `gc.log`. If it is (2), the detector needs a precondition assert that distinguishes \"git chose not to run maintenance\" from \"maintenance ran but was not a descendant\" — today both produce the same empty `offenders` and the same opaque message.\n4. Regardless of cause, improve the failure message: it currently prints `offenders` (empty) and cannot distinguish those two cases, which is why this report cannot name the mechanism.\n\n## Scope\nIn scope: `tests/unit/test_store_commit_maintenance_bd66.py`, and a test-artifact-preservation helper if one is needed to capture the trace. Out of scope: production code, the `_AUTOMAINT_OFF` suppression, `_GIT_TIMEOUT`, and the explicit maintenance step — the production invariant is not implicated by this failure.\n\n## Testing\n`pytest tests/unit/test_store_commit_maintenance_bd66.py -q` and under load `-n 4`, repeated; then `make verify`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The failure is reproduced at least once with the Trace2 file and `gc.log` preserved, and the ticket records which of the three candidate mechanisms the trace shows.\n- [ ] The detector distinguishes, in its failure message, \"no maintenance process started at all\" from \"a maintenance process started but was not a descendant of a commit\".\n- [ ] The identified cause is fixed at the class level, with no wall-clock bound and no retry/rerun of the assertion.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/test_store_commit_maintenance_bd66.py -q -n 4` passes on 10 consecutive runs on a loaded host.\n- [ ] `make verify` exits 0.","id":"ab03-0276-374b-46af","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Flaky: the bd66 maintenance DETECTOR test fails under full-suite load (the test added to fix a flake)"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793080273974766321,"uuid":"271e05b3-b30b-46fc-8919-38bcbd5e6219"}